WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:11.700
A hierarchical file system can be in a data set type called HFS or it can be in a ZFS data set.

2
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:17.700
HFS data sets can span many density volumes. If spanned, they must be SMS managed.

3
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:23.700
ZFS and HFS are both Unix file systems and both can participate in shared systplexes.

4
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.700
However, while HFS always has a single file system per data set,

5
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.700
ZFS can have multiple file systems in a single data set.

6
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:36.700
These data sets are called aggregates and are a collection of data sets.

7
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:42.700
HFS data sets are like PDSE data sets. They're allocated like any other ZOS data set.

8
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.700
Although an HFS data set is a ZOS data set and MVS open request fails,

9
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:53.700
users must use Unix system services to access data in such a data set.

10
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:59.700
The hierarchical file system consists of one root file system and multiple user file systems.

11
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.700
The root file system is the base for HFS and is at the top of the hierarchy.

12
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:10.700
HFS data sets are allocated as ZOS data sets, so DSN type equals HFS.

13
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.700
HFS data sets can only be opened by Unix system service kernel operations

14
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:21.700
and HFS data sets can reside on the same volumes as other ZOS data sets.

15
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.700
Non-SMS managed HFS data sets are often used.

16
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:32.700
Now, a few other characteristics. The size of the data set is limited to two gigabytes if single volume.

17
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.700
A multi-volume HFS can span up to a maximum of 59 volumes.

18
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.700
HFS can have up to a maximum of 123 extents per volume

19
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:48.700
and a multi-volume HFS can have up to a maximum of 255 total extents for all volumes.

20
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.700
Also, keep in mind that within a file system in HFS,

21
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.700
there is no way to control the amount of space each user can use.

22
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.700
It's actually possible for one user to use the entire space in a file system.

23
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.700
So the recommendation here is that each user have their own file system.

24
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:12.700
A ZFS aggregate is a VSAM linear data set or an LDS that contains one or more ZFS file systems.

25
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.700
The name of the aggregate is its VSAM LDS name.

26
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.700
A ZVS file system is a logical named entity contained within the ZVS aggregate

27
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.700
that has a root directory and can be mounted.

28
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.700
It has a logical maximum size known as its quota.

29
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:32.700
A ZFS file system is a named entity that resides in a ZFS aggregate.

30
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.700
It contains a root directory and can be mounted into the USS hierarchy.

31
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.700
File system is not really a new term, but a ZFS file system resides in a ZFS aggregate,

32
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.700
which is different from an HFS file system.

33
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:53.700
For compatibility mode aggregates, the VSAM LDS name is the same as the ZFS aggregate name,

34
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.700
is the same as the ZFS file system name.

35
00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.700
The quota of these file systems is set to the size of the aggregate.

36
00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.700
A compatibility mode aggregate can contain only one ZFS file system,

37
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.700
making this type of aggregate more like an HFS file system.

38
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.700
This is flagged in the aggregate when it's created.

39
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.700
Version file systems in a shared file system that are no longer being used

40
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.700
can be automatically unmounted.

41
00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:23.700
Also, note that the multi-file system aggregates are no longer supported by ZOS.

42
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:31.700
A colony address space runs USS system code as an extension to the USS kernel address space.

43
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.700
ZFS runs as a Unix system services colony address space.

44
00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.700
There must be an entry in a bpx-prm-palmlib member for ZVS,

45
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.700
and the ZVS proc must be available.

46
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:47.700
The bpx-palm member in the palmlib contains a file-sys type statement

47
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.700
for the ZVS physical file system.

48
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.700
It includes an AS name parameter specifying the ZFS proc.

49
00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:59.700
AS name is required for PFSs that run in colony address spaces.

50
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:04.700
ZFS can now handle mounts of ZFS file systems contained in the bpx-palm.

51
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:11.700
Mount commands can be left in slash etc slash rc or moved into bpx-palm.

52
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.700
ZFS admin commands the query information, for example,

53
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.700
lsfs and aggrinfo can be issued by any user.

54
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.700
ZFS admin commands that modify, so for example,

55
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:28.700
ZFS admin create require that the user be logged in as root.

56
00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.700
Now, what are the advantages of ZFS?

57
00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.700
Well, ZFS is a file system that is fundamentally different from HFS.

58
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.700
The administration view of ZFS is different from HFS.

59
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.700
ZFS file systems are created in a different manner

60
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:47.700
from the way that HFS file systems are created.

61
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.700
But in general, however, the application view of ZFS is the same

62
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.700
as the application view of HFS.

63
00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:01.700
The same APIs and commands are used for ZFS as are used for HFS.

64
00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.700
Once the ZFS file system is mounted,

65
00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:09.700
it's almost indistinguishable from a mounted HFS file system.

66
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:13.700
ZFS is a ZOS Unix file system that can be used in addition

67
00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.700
to the hierarchical file system.

68
00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.700
ZFS file systems contain files and directories that can be accessed

69
00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.700
with the ZOS hierarchical file system

70
00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.700
file application programming interfaces or APIs.

71
00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.700
ZFS file systems can be mounted into the ZOS Unix hierarchy

72
00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.700
along with other local or remote file system types,

73
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:38.700
for example, HFS, TFS, AutoMount, NFS, and so forth.

74
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.700
ZFS does not replace HFS, rather it's complementary to it.

75
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:50.700
And importantly, ZFS is the strategic Unix system services file system for ZOS.

76
00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.700
Now, in the next video, we'll take a closer look

77
00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.700
at using the HFS and ZFS file systems.

78
00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.700
ZFS file systems contain file systems.
